[medium] 4-5 segments[4].narration
    wrong: Add her pushes, about four points, to the average advertiser's score, then pass the total through the S-curve: 5 percent becomes 73.
    why:   Two sentences earlier the average advertiser is given a probability of 'about 5 percent' (0.047 in facts.json explain_examples), and audit_checklist[2] in this lesson uses 'score' to mean probability. Adding +4 log-odds points to 0.047 and passing the sum through the S-curve gives 98.3%, not 72.7%; her ~4 scorecard (log-odds) points must be added to the average advertiser's pre-S-curve scorecard p
    fix:   Add her pushes, about four points, to the average advertiser's scorecard points, then pass the total through the S-curve: 5 percent becomes 73.
[medium] 4-5 segments[4].visual.note
    wrong: Average advertiser: 4.7%. Her pushes added to its score, then through the S-curve: 72.7%.
    why:   Immediately after 'Average advertiser: 4.7%', 'its score' points to 4.7% (0.047, a probability already past the S-curve). Adding ~4 log-odds points to 0.047 before applying the S-curve gives 98.3%, not 72.7%; the pushes are added to the average advertiser's pre-S-curve scorecard points (log-odds), not to 4.7%.
    fix:   Average advertiser: 4.7%. Her pushes added to the average advertiser's scorecard points, then through the S-curve: 72.7%.
